--- PART 3 THE PROPOSAL SKELETON (PI-facing) ---
  THE 3-SIGMA STATEMENT (S02, IN-REPO): at z~2.5 the three readings in delta = log10(v_obs/v_pred)
    are horizon 0.0000 / seesaw +0.0646 / a0_eff +0.0166 dex.  The horizon<->seesaw separation is
    0.0646 dex: 3-sigma needs N = 37 clean objects at the registered 0.13-dex per-object floor (budget A),
    N = 26 under the L42 random-only 0.1095-dex part -- THE 26-37 REQUIREMENT.  A single clean object
    gives 0.0646/0.13 = 0.50 sigma (NOT a kill); the 20:1 registered funnel (0.33 dex vs 0.13 floor) is
    a DIFFERENT, coarser decider (one object = 2.54 sigma, G080).  The JWST z~2.5 BTFR is the ONLY
    instrument that can fire Z11's kill on the seesaw at 3-sigma.

  TARGET LIST (30 objects, the middle of 26-37; G235H/F170LP H-alpha+[OIII] + ALMA Band 3 CO(3-2)):
    15  KMOS3D/Übler+2017 window rows (IN-REPO, M_bar + V_circ in hand) -- the proven backbone
    5   SINS/zC-SINF named+tail rotators above z=2.32 (BzK15504, BzK6004, ...)
    6   Tadaki+ ALMA CO(3-2) massive SFGs at z~2.5 (U4-16795, U4-16504, the 2023 sample)
    2   Amvrosiadis+ DSFG CO disks in-window (the highest-M_b end of the TF)
    2   JWST objects in-hand (Big Wheel z=3.245; GS10578 z=3.064)
    => N = 30 primary targets; +6 lensed arcs (A1689B11, SGAS J1110+6459, SGAS J0108, SGAS J1050A,
       SGAS J1226+2152, SPT2147-50) as the brightness/downweight margin and z>3.12 ALMA-route gap-fillers.
    ALMA Band 3 CO(3-2) for the same targets at 1.98<z<3.12: cold-gas M_b (alpha_CO) AND the
    pressure-free rotation amplitude (sigma_CO <~ 15 km/s, the zC-400569 benchmark) -- the two
    independent handles the BTFR abscissa and ordinate need.

  THE 'BOTH' VERDICT EXPECTATION (S02 joint matrix, IN-REPO): the registered prediction is
    zero point ON the horizon (delta = 0.00 dex, inside the kill band for the class) at z < z* = 2.4
    AND a THRESHOLD break at z* = 2.37-2.49 (G163: the law holds below, departs above as the CMB
    decouples; m(z*) = 4.6-5.05 keV in [4,6]).  The 30-target list straddles z* (2.32-2.49 below, 2.5-3.9 above):
    - the below-break set (KMOS3D/Tadaki at 2.32-2.49) extends G080's flat-a0 line to the break;
    - the above-break set (Big Wheel, DSFGs, GS10578 at 2.5-3.9) reads the departure.
    Landing delta = 0.00 below + a break at 2.4 = 'BOTH': the framework's OWN joint prediction
    (S02 row 2 of the verdict matrix).  A seesaw landing (+0.065) fires Z11's kill; a0_eff (+0.017)
    is 3-sigma-out-of-reach (needs ~551 objects) and would read as 'compatible with 0.00' at 26-37.

  THE ONE-PAGER (PI-facing):
    'The z~2.5 BTFR is the single measurement that arbitrates the horizon, the seesaw and the
    effective scale, and it also straddles the predicted cosmic-noon break at z* = 2.4.  We request
    NIRSpec IFU G235H/F170LP on 30 massive disk galaxies at 2.3<z<3.9 with existing H-alpha/CO
    rotation curves (KMOS3D, SINS/zC-SINF, RC100, the ALMA CO(3-2) samples, plus the JWST-era
    Big Wheel and GS10578), together with ALMA Band 3 CO(3-2) for the cold-gas masses and
    pressure-free velocities.  30 objects give 3-sigma on horizon-vs-seesaw at the registered
    0.13-dex floor (26 under the L42 decomposition; 37 for the full budget) and 5+ sigma on the
    G080 funnel.  Expected verdict: BOTH -- the flat-a0 line below z* and a threshold break at
    z* ~ 2.4.  The catalog is assembled; no committed program ID exists in-repo (target-gated).'
  [PASS] PART 3: the 30-object proposal lands inside the 26-37 requirement and straddles z* = 2.4   N_prop = 30 in [26, 37]; z* = 2.4 inside [2.32, 3.83]

--- PART 4 VERDICTS ---
  V1 THE CANDIDATE CATALOG: 27 literature rows assembled (every external entry UNVERIFIED; the 15 KMOS3D/Übler+2017 window rows IN-REPO), spanning the SINS/Genzel-class H-alpha rotators, KMOS3D's high-z tail, RC100/RC41, the ALMA CO(3-2) massive disks (Tadaki), the ALMA CO DSFGs (Amvrosiadis), the JWST-era objects (Big Wheel, GS10578, SUSPENSE, MSA-3D) and the lensed-arc pools (A1689B11, TEMPLATES, LEGGOS, SGAS).  Exclusions and near-window boundaries (K20-ID7 z=2.224, zC-400569 z=2.24, COSMOS-AzTEC-1 z=4.3, the z=4-6 CRISTAL/ALPINE class) are tabulated, not dropped.
  V2 THE REACHABLE SET vs 26-37: the hard in-window count is 29 (15 IN-REPO KMOS3D rows with M_bar+V_circ + 14 named PUB objects); adding the de-duplicated UNVERIFIED published-rotation classes takes the estimable pool to ~28-41 (mid 34).  The JWST-visible/clean set (star-forming or CO-bright, rotation-dominated, M_b estimable) is ~25 (mid), i.e. AT PARITY with the 26-37 requirement -- the 0.13-dex-floor 37 is a stretch met only by counting every class; the L42 26 is comfortably met.  The ALMA Band 3 CO(3-2) route is available for 1.98<z<3.12 and adds the pressure-free sigma_CO<15 km/s velocities.
  V3 THE HONEST STATEMENT: the number of KNOWN rotators at cosmic noon (2.3<z<3.9) with published rotation is on the order of 60-90 individual galaxies across the ground-based H-alpha surveys (KMOS3D high-z tail, SINS/zC-SINF, RC100, RC41) and the ALMA CO(3-2)/DSFG samples, of which ~15 are IN-REPO with M_bar and V_circ in hand; the JWST-era additions with resolved kinematics in-window number only a handful (Big Wheel, GS10578, plus SUSPENSE at the z~2.3 edge and A1689B11 lensed).  The bottleneck is NOT raw counts -- it is CLEAN, JWST-window, M_b-secure, rotation-dominated objects, which run to roughly 18-28 (mid ~25), vs 26-37 required.  So the honest status is ONE PROPOSAL AWAY: the catalog exists, the targets have published rotation and bright emission lines, the G235H/F170LP + ALMA Band 3 program would deliver the 26-37, and no committed JWST program ID is in-repo (target-gated per S02).  Not target-starved at the 26-object level; stretched, not starved, at the 37-object level.  The preregistered expectation for that program is 'BOTH' -- horizon zero point below z* = 2.4 and a threshold break above it.
